# 🧬 Molecular Docking
---
Overview:
---
This directory contains work carried out as part of a Molecular Docking component of a bioinformatics training program.

Molecular docking is a computational approach used to investigate how a small molecule (ligand) may interact with a biological macromolecule, typically a protein target. The technique predicts plausible binding orientations (poses) and provides computational estimates of binding affinity that can be used to explore potential protein–ligand interactions.

The work presented in this section demonstrates the application of computational structural biology concepts to protein–ligand interaction analysis and drug discovery.

---
🎯 Objectives:
---
The major objectives of the molecular docking work were to:

* Understand the principles and applications of molecular docking.
* Prepare protein and ligand structures for computational analysis.
* Identify and define appropriate binding regions on protein targets.
* Perform protein–ligand docking simulations.
* Examine predicted ligand binding poses and interactions.
* Interpret docking scores as computational measures of binding affinity.
* Visualize protein–ligand complexes in three dimensions.
* Develop practical skills in computational drug discovery and structural bioinformatics.

---
🔬 Molecular Docking Workflow:
---
The general workflow followed in this project can be summarized as:
Protein Structure
       │
       ▼
Protein Preparation
       │
       ▼
Binding Site / Grid Definition
       │
       │
Ligand Structure ──► Ligand Preparation
       │
       ▼
Protein–Ligand Docking
       │
       ▼
Docking Poses & Scores
